Date: Wed, 13 Oct 93 15:01:21 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: SPACE: International Space Enterprises - Prospectus

Tony Hamilton asks: 
> ... any comments? 

I've made extended comments on this type of thing to sci.space,
which I can repost here if there's demand for this sort of thing,
or you can order a collection through NCAT (p1000 for custom 
orders).  Personally, I am sick and tired of arguing with greedy 
government pork barrel hypesters and their cult fans, and would 
rather be spared that sort of thing here.

To briefly review: this kind of project is based on
expectation of future _government_ funding.  As space 
tech costs decrease, there are a large number of space markets 
that become lucrative well before viable commercial use of the moon.  
A rational commercial venture picks the "low hanging fruit" first.  

Lunar transport is the equivalent of California pioneers in the
mid 1800's proposing to spend big $$$ on a transport system to get borax 
out of Death Valley, while ignoring the construction of the mines 
themselves, not to mention completely ignoring the fact that mining gold
on the Sacremento would be easier to do and vastly more
lucrative.  Thus, the notion that this is a "commercial" project 
is fairly transparently bogus, although it may not be so obvious 
to people who've spent most of their careers in the government 
contracting world.

Furthermore, I'm afraid these ISE folks won't have much inside
track on government contracts unless they deeply embed themselves
in the U.S. and European military-industrial complex, the
only major source of government space project funding.
A comparable project is Spacehab: hyped as a "commercial"
project, it never did have any significant commercial market,
and ended up being bought out by companies whose main value-add
the ability to effectively lobby for pork.  They were
give fairly normal NASA contracts under the guise of 
"commercialization". 

To turn ISE into a more commercial project there's an interesting
direction they could go, towards proprietary exploration of
more visually interesting alien environments (Mars, Jupiter system,
comets, etc) and the sale of this data as virtual sets for movies, 
VR games, virtual tourism, science, educational material, etc.
Such a business might evolve into more extensive scientific prospecting 
for a wide variety of useful native materials.  Cf. my comet mining 
post for such a long-term space industrialization scenario (p200), which 
is more lucrative and much easier than scenarios based on lunar
mining.

