With the new #2 it looks like I've got the thing worded so that it fits
in with most people's idea of "will"/"free will"/"volition" or whatever
it's to be called.  I've realised that the phrase "appears to return the
highest value" is a bit of a fudge-factor.  The neo-determinists will
say that appearances can be deceptive, and hence illusory.  I'd go on to
further qualify the phrase:

We could never claim to be incapable of making mistakes, with every
decision turning out to be truly the "highest value", itself a subjective
notion.  On balance, though, individuals' subjective notions of what is
"good for" them are more likely to be fairly accurate than hopelessly
false.  And the individuals have to be free to adjust their values in #2,
so each individual's judgement is what counts.  It has to be a subjective
evaluation.  The emergence of consciousness suggests that those who had
use of it tended to make more good decisions than bad decisions, so
objectively this is consistent with subjective appearances.  So "appears
to return the highest value" is a correct assessment most of the time;
otherwise it's a mistake (or an illusion).

I'd almost be satisfied with #2 after pointing this out, although I'd
like to go rather further than this and eliminate strict determinism and
predestination.  This has already been achieved with quantum mechanics,
so the Calvinists are refuted.  My "will" #2 = "will" #3.

The sort of universe that Einstein fought for, a monotrack relativistic
universe, is strictly deterministic.  Everything runs like clockwork,
"God does not play dice", but this instantly rules out any sort of free
will (probably why these types of reality are not seen -- intelligent
life would not have evolved, and if it ever found itself in such a
predicament would vote with its feet.  That is, if it still could!)  All
future events would be an "inevitable consequence of all the preceding
events", their world-lines frozen in place with everything powerless to
avoid its fate like a clockwork train running along a single track, or a
puppet on a string.  A universe set up by a "God" or giant Mussolini.

This was shown to be a mistaken world-view over 60 years ago.  It's not
very important as the future is hidden from us.  My volition #3 is just
the icing on the cake.  Let's say I'm about to make a decision -- A or
B.  I choose B, and subsequently my former duplicates in reality space
have now become just that.  The A's are near-duplicates, and continuing
to diverge.  People might say, "Ah, but some of your previous selves
took the A route -- you might have gone along that road."  But I'm not
interested in that, I took the action that _I_ selected, and at any
point in time I will have made all the decisions that I made in the past,
and diverged accordingly.  And this also applies to everybody else.   

	      >Although the hunger feelings may act as a
	      >trigger you're still free to ignore them for one hour .
	       
>If I was free to ignore them why didn't I ? Because I didn't
>want to. why? ....  The point is it had a cause.
	
Let's say that you put someone in a particular situation S with their
brain in a state B.  They make a decision D.  Now if they're put in the
same situation again with the extra experiences the brain is no longer
in state B, but if you could then reset the brain back to state B and
exactly replicate the situation S then they should make decision D
again, although there might be some quantum randomness.  I wouldn't
complain if they made decision D every time, since this was genuinely
their choice.  Now you might remark that we're just responding to
environment all the time, and there's no room for freedom.  But part
of the time we're not forced to respond to the environment -- we can
just sit down and think about what we want to do, and make plans.  So
the next time situation S comes up the brain is in a new state R and
the decision is to go for E.

So the cause is partly environment, and partly how we've chosen to
program ourselves, but I think this gives us enough freedom to make
"Executive decisions".  With the Many Minds Interpretation it's just
a bonus to know that we aren't lumbered with predestination, but I
don't think we need to claim any more "free" will than this. 

>		  > I'd say that the drug would have reduced the
