hemispheres, so the first traces would be shown in the early mammals,
then it would take on a much greater control in primates.
	      
		>for our example of a conscious mind this [ input / output
		>relationship] is only useful if we presuppose that all the      
		>capabilities of a self-conscious intelligent being can be 
		>reduced to an algorithm,
		 
>We should remember the immortal words of Socrates
>( or was it Plato ?) who said " If it looks like a duck, walks
>like a duck , quacks like a duck then its a duck".

What -- an operationalist philosopher?  Sounds unlikely  :-)

In the case of Searle's "Chinese Room" we can easily imagine characters
being manipulated, but it's harder or impossible to imagine the whole
system having a structure so complex that it could comprehend the
meaning of its thoughts.  But this works the other way, and in
functionalism it's easy to imagine something that appears to "quack
like a duck" but difficult to imagine all the very subtle things that
could distinguish it from a duck.

>I suppose it
>might somehow be  possible that parallel processing could
>generate new subjective experiences of some sort but now your
>leaving science and entering meta- physics .

Here's a couple of qualitative distinctions of parallel processing: 

(1) The system can generalize acquired knowledge to new situations.
(2) The system can sustain minor damage with little or no loss of
function, and increasing damage causes a gradual degradation rather
than a catastrophic crash.  

The brain works by parallel processing, so it's reasonable to assume
that an equivalently architectured machine could produce all the
features we want -- genuine consciousness, will, etc.  I suppose serial
processing might work in principle, but I'd be concerned about other
distinctions in addition to speed.  (Still if you built a machine
that was as slow as a meatbrain, but serial and so slower by six orders
of magnitude this would suit those who got bored on long intergalactic
trips.  Creating your own basement universes should be no problem, so
the time loss would be no big deal, although I'd probably forsake
throwing away much time until I'd proved that moving into a basement
universe was as easy as ABC.  Living on Earth would be like a Twilight
Zone -- flicker from day/night just too fast to see at 12Hz, and having
to move house every "hour" before they were condemned.)
 
>I think the heart of the question of the Will can be found in
