Alright, since you asked so politely, here it is:

                            *   *   *

               "The Ethiopians and the Skyjackers"

This story was told to me by a pilot for a major US airline.  He
said the story was known--and believed--by everyone in the
airline industry.  If it is not true, it still may be the reason
no Ethiopian airliners were skyjacked for nearly two decades.
And if it isn't true, it should be.

Those of you who are old enough will remember the epidemic of
airliner skyjackings in the early '70s.  It seemed as though
every week or so, some terrorist group or another was skyjacking
a plane, beating and murdering hostages, blowing up jets, etc.
The US response was to put "Sky Marshals" on all US flights.  In
addition, special anti-skyjacking quick response teams were
created.  Some countries had their own programs; others didn't.

The Ethiopians didn't have any specific programs, but the plane
crews were encouraged to take appropriate action to neutralize
any skyjack attempts.  As it happened, this "no-plan plan" worked
the very first time an Ethiopian airliner was threatened.  When
terrorists tried to take over a long international flight, the
crew and the passengers were able to disarmed and subdued them.
What happened next, set the trend for twenty years of Ethiopian
"friendly skies."

The crew reasoned that imprisoned skyjackers were terrorism
magnates.  They would draw other violent attacks aimed at
"liberating" their comrades.  In addition, even a trial that
ended in death for the skyjackers would easily last long enough
for many terrorist attacks to take place before the miscreants
were executed.  Such reasoning suggested its own solution.

The captured skyjackers were taken to the first class section of
the plane and tied to a row of seats.  They were draped in
blankets and towels were wrapped around their necks.  One by one,
the terrorists had their throat slit by a crew member.  Upon
landing in Ethiopia, the authorities were given the bodies along
with an explanation as to what the crew had done.

The crew was rewarded; no one was prosecuted; and no Ethiopian
airliner was skyjacked again until the early '90s.

